WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.390
There's nothing worse than watching a trailer
for an upcoming movie you're excited about—only

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.300
to realize halfway through that the trailer
is giving away the entire plot.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.679
And it isn't just a modern problem, either.

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:14.240
Marketing companies have been putting major
spoilers in trailers for years.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.320
Let's take a look at some trailers that totally
ruined the movie.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.690
And, well, spoilers ahead, obviously.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.140
Last House on the Left

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.310
The trailer for 2009's Last House on the Left
leaves no surprise unspoiled as it bounces

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.200
through the entire script.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.040
In the space of two and a half minutes, we
learn that two young women have an unfortunate

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.480
run-in with fugitives, who then execute one
and leave the other one in a lake.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.329
As they look for a place to hide overnight,
the gang ends up at the lake house of the

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:42.329
girl they shot.

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:45.880
If the trailer had stopped there, it would
have been pretty good—unfortunately, it

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.979
goes on to show the surviving girl making
it home, and her parents exacting their revenge

00:00:49.979 --> 00:00:54.079
on the criminals…including the literal final
scene of the movie.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:56.210
There's really no point in even watching it
now.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.750
You know everything that happens.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:00:59.780
Cast Away

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.679
It may be one of Tom Hanks' most celebrated
roles, but anyone who saw the trailer for

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:06.980
Cast Away promptly had the entire plot spoiled
for them.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.220
In the preview, we're shown the plane crash
Hanks' character survives and his arrival

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.110
on the island where he's stranded.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.620
Standard trailer stuff.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.150
Really makes you wonder if the guy survives,
huh?

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.120
Well...just keep watching the trailer!

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.160
It shows Hanks' character building a raft
and writing a note detailing how long he'd

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.360
been on the island.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.780
Even worse, it inexplicably tells us that
he survives this harrowing journey and makes

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.090
it back home.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.660
The Cast Away trailer is also another one
of those previews that uses the last shot

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.920
of the movie as the last shot of the trailer,
so there are literally no surprises left.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.780
Thanks for ruining everything, trailer editors.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.280
"I… have made fire!"

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.000
Yup, even that.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.170
Shutter Island

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.600
When the first trailer for Shutter Island
was released, Paramount Pictures didn't bother

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.820
to hide much of the plot.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.290
In fact, the trailer pretty much neatly summarized
the entire thing.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:01.500
It becomes evident early on that the disappearance
of Rachel Solando is not the real mystery

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.050
here, but the existence of an unknown 67th
patient at the mental institution and the

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:10.140
quickly accelerating deterioration of the
main character's grip on reality.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:16.080
Thankfully, the trailer left the big, super
twisty twist at the end intact for filmgoers,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.780
but that that point, you could pretty much
forget about enjoying the ride.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.460
The Sum of All Fears

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.750
The trailer for Ben Affleck's debut as Jack
Ryan in 2002's The Sum of All Fears is a lesson

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.530
in how NOT to do a trailer for a spy film.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.620
This clip sets up the main threat early on:
will Affleck and Morgan Freeman be able to

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.160
track down the missing Russian scientists
before they can build a nuclear bomb and use

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.420
it in the U.S.?

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:43.230
That sounds like an intriguing plot, and might
have drawn a casual viewer to see the movie.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:47.650
Unfortunately, the trailer then destroys all
the suspenseful build-up by showing the bomb's

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:48.680
detonation.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.010
The rest of the story, where Ryan keeps the
President from wrongly retaliating against

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.140
Russia, seems like a major letdown after such
a big reveal.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.250
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:05.209
Naturally, there were several trailers for
2016's mega-movie Batman v Superman: Dawn

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:06.209
of Justice.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:10.130
But with the second promo trailer, the studio
apparently decided to just give away the entire

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.200
plot and hope fans were too devoted to the
brand to care.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.680
The trailer makes no mystery of most of the
critical plot points, with scenes in order

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.660
and all the major beats revealed: Lex Luthor
introduces Bruce Wayne and Clark Kent, then

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.150
manipulates them both to get them to fight.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.720
The two superheroes duke it out for a while,
which is in the title, so that's fine.. until

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.750
surprise!—here's Doomsday to fight them.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.040
But wait—who appears to save the day?

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.410
Why it's Wonder Woman, and the duo becomes
a trio—working togethecr to battle the bad

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.099
guy and save the world.

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:47.660
Despite all the spoilers, the movie made over
$870 million at the worldwide box office,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.570
so apparently the strategy paid off.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:52.040
Final Destination

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.490
All the films in the Final Destination series
are pretty bad about putting huge spoilers

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.870
in their trailers.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.030
While we know the entire premise of the movies
revolves around the demise of people who cheated

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.959
fate, spoiling the characters' comeuppance
in the trailers pretty much defeats the purpose.

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:12.629
In the trailer for the first Final Destination
movie alone, we get to see Tod, Ms. Lewton,

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:14.849
and Billy meet their gruesome fates.

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:19.690
Later installments in the franchise are similarly
spoilery, usually revealing the demise of

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:22.680
at least a third of the cast along the way.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.020
Kingsman: The Golden Circle

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:30.070
In 2014's Kingsmanc: The Secret Service, Colin
Firth's character Harry Hart is shot in the

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.840
face by Valentine.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.690
It's implied that Hart is out of the picture,
with Eggsy taking over his role within the

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.410
Kingsman spy organization.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.660
With the first official trailer for The Golden
Circle, the Fox marketing team decided to

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.480
spoil a huge secret in the final seconds of
the trailer:

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.220
"Oh my God…

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.220
Harry?"

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:53.250
This revelation didn't sit well with director
Matthew Vaughn, who said:

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.870
"I begged the studio not to reveal it.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.280
Because it’s the whole driving force of
the first act and if you didn’t know that

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.410
scene it would’ve made the whole audience
gasp."

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.221
Apparently, the marketing guys didn't take
the hint, because the second trailer for The

00:05:06.221 --> 00:05:11.530
Golden Circle showed even Harry in action,
along with a reveal of Julianne Moore's character

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.530
as the big bad.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.220
Friday the 13th

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.280
Although the 1980 horror film Friday the 13th
eventually went on to become a massive box-office

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.030
success and spawned an entire franchise, the
trailer for the movie definitely didn't do

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:26.310
it any favors.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:30.960
In typical '80s style, the trailer for Friday
the 13th has a voiceover—but this time,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.200
the marketing team at Paramount didn't bother
trying to entice audiences with a mysterious

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.919
backstory or synopsis.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:41.580
Instead, they just showed every character's
demise while the voiceover counts them.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.640
"One."

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.960
Not only does the trailer have no qualms about
spoiling every twist in the movie, it also

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.430
takes a weird turn—in counting up to the
13 in the title, the trailer implies that

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:58.669
there will be 12 casualties during the film,
but there were actually only ten: the nine

00:05:58.669 --> 00:06:01.360
victims and Pamela Voorhees herself.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.320
So much for truth in advertising.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:04.680
Thanks for watching!

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.050
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.100
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

